Make the SSE guard per-architecture rather than per-configure-run.

A universal Mach-O build compiles every slice from a single configure
run, but configure probes the host only once. On an Apple Silicon runner
the "working SSE intrinsics" test compiles _mm_sfence() and fails, so
HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H is left undefined for *both* slices -- yet the x86_64
slice still compiles the SSE code path and references
init_xrpow_core_sse, which was never built:

    Undefined symbols for architecture x86_64:
      "_init_xrpow_core_sse"

Seeding ac_cv_header_xmmintrin_h does not help. The check is a
hand-rolled AC_COMPILE_IFELSE that assigns the variable unconditionally
(configure line ~13411), not an AC_CHECK_HEADER that consults the cache,
so a pre-seeded value is overwritten before it is ever read.

Gate both the definition and its use on __SSE__, which the compiler
defines per-slice as it compiles. The two then appear and disappear
together whichever architecture is being built, and single-architecture
builds are unaffected.

--- a/libmp3lame/quantize.c
+++ b/libmp3lame/quantize.c
@@ -94,7 +94,7 @@
 {
     gfc->init_xrpow_core = init_xrpow_core_c;
 
-#if defined(HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H)
+#if defined(HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H) && defined(__SSE__)
     if (gfc->CPU_features.SSE)
         gfc->init_xrpow_core = init_xrpow_core_sse;
 #endif
--- a/libmp3lame/vector/xmm_quantize_sub.c
+++ b/libmp3lame/vector/xmm_quantize_sub.c
@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@
 
 
 
-#ifdef HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H
+#if defined(HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H) || defined(__SSE__)
 
 #include <xmmintrin.h>
 
